var tafsirData_80 = {
  "1": "The opening verse of Sūrah ‘Abasa takes us directly into a moment of profound divine correction. It says of the Prophet (ﷺ) that “He frowned and turned his attention away.\n\n” The word “frowned” translates the Arabic ‘abasa, a facial expression showing mild displeasure or constriction, while the turning away indicates a shift of focus. The background, reported in a well-known ḥadīth collected by At-Tirmidhī and others, is that the Prophet (ﷺ) was earnestly engaged in conversation with a group of influential Meccan leaders, hoping to bring them to Islam. During this delicate moment, ‘Abdullāh ibn Umm Maktūm, a blind Muslim eager for guidance, approached and repeatedly interrupted, unaware of the ongoing discussion. The Prophet’s natural human reaction was to show a flicker of discomfort and to continue addressing the elite pagans, as he deeply desired their acceptance of the truth for the benefit of the whole community. This verse captures that subtle, unspoken response. What makes it so remarkable is that the Qur’ān records it not as a sin but as a lesson for all time. The Lord of the Worlds did not overlook even this minor neglect of a sincere seeker. This teaches us that no matter how noble one’s intentions, spiritual priorities must remain clear. Those who come with humility and need should never be sidelined for those who are self-sufficient and indifferent.\n\nThe verse also establishes the absolute honesty of the divine revelation; it does not hide the human moments of the Messenger but uses them to elevate his character and instruct the believers. Thus, a simple frown becomes the opening of a sūrah that realigns the priorities of da‘wah, reminding us that the worth of a person is not measured by social standing but by the eagerness of the heart to be purified. It is a gentle yet firm opening that immediately draws the listener’s attention to the timeless value of mercy and accessibility in teaching the faith.",
  "2": "The second verse reveals the specific reason behind the Prophet’s reaction: “simply because the blind man came to him interrupting.” The Arabic construction uses an interrogative particle to ask, “What would make you know?” but the meaning is that the only cause for frowning was the blind man’s interruption. ‘Abdullāh ibn Umm Maktūm, being blind, could not see the Prophet’s preoccupation with the Quraysh nobles, nor could he perceive the subtle cues of the situation. His repeated calls, “Teach me from what Allah has taught you,” came from a burning desire for guidance. The verse implies that the Prophet turned away solely because the man’s timing seemed inconvenient, not because of any disdain for the man himself.\n\nYet Allah, the Most Merciful, wanted to etch in the consciousness of the Ummah that physical blindness and social inconvenience are never barriers to the mercy of the message. The man who was blind in his eyes possessed a heart full of insight, and his interruption, however untimely it appeared, was in reality a test of prophetic attentiveness. This rebuke is exceptionally beautiful because it shows that the divine standard prioritises spiritual need over worldly calculations. The one who came seeking to be purified was implicitly more valuable in that moment than those who were indifferent.\n\nThe verse also underscores that the sincerity of the seeker transforms a seeming interruption into a blessed opportunity. It is as if the revelation is saying that no moment spent with a genuine student of faith is ever wasted, even if it means momentarily turning away from the powerful. For all callers to truth, this remains a foundational instruction: never underestimate the person who comes to you “interrupting” because their urgency may be a sign of the very eagerness that Allah loves.",
  "3": "With gentle words, the third verse tells the Prophet, “You never know, O Prophet, perhaps he may be purified.” This is a profound teaching about the limits of human judgment.\n\nThe phrase “you never know” is not merely a statement of ignorance but a redirection of the Prophet’s perception. Even the most discerning of human beings cannot peer into the ultimate destiny of a soul. The blind man, ‘Abdullāh ibn Umm Maktūm, might have seemed, from a purely worldly perspective, a simple man with little influence, but his inner readiness was known only to Allah.\n\nThe word “purified” here translates yazzakkā, which conveys spiritual cleansing, growth, and the shedding of sins. The verse suggests that the very act of seeking knowledge is a means of purification, and the one who approaches the teacher with a sincere heart is on the brink of being cleansed. The Prophet’s eagerness to invite the powerful chiefs was rooted in a noble hope that their conversion would bring many others, but the divine wisdom reminds him that the purification of a single eager soul is a treasure in itself.\n\nThis verse also dismantles any notion that outward status can indicate who deserves the message. In contemporary application, it compels every teacher, every caller to faith, and indeed every believer, to look beyond appearances and social labels. No one can tell whose heart is prepared to receive the light. The “perhaps” leaves the outcome with Allah, meaning the teacher’s duty is to offer the reminder without prejudging who is worthy. The beauty of this instruction is that it liberates us from the tyranny of results; we are not responsible for who becomes purified, but we are responsible for not turning away those who come seeking. The uncertainty in “perhaps” also guards against arrogance, keeping the teacher humble, always aware that the ultimate transformation of hearts is entirely in the hands of the Almighty.",
  "4": "Building directly on the previous hope, the fourth verse completes the picture: “or he may be mindful, benefitting from the reminder.” The Arabic word yadhdhakkaru means to remember, to take heed, to actively recall what one inherently knows about the Creator. The blind man was not simply asking for dry information; he was seeking a reminder that would awaken his soul.\n\nThe term “reminder,” here dhikrā, refers to the revelation itself, which serves as a powerful admonition for those with living hearts. The verse presents two parallel possibilities: that he may be purified, and that he may be mindful and thus draw benefit from the reminder. Together, they illustrate the complete process of spiritual learning: internal purification and the active engagement of the mind and heart with the truth. What is striking is that the benefit of the reminder is not automatic; it depends on the mindfulness of the recipient. The Prophet, by turning away, was in effect delaying this potential benefit for a man who was fully primed to receive it. This verse thus reinforces the error being corrected: the one who was indifferent, the pagan chief, was not in a state of mindfulness, so the reminder would not yet benefit him, whereas the eager blind man was entirely ready. The lesson for all believers is profound. When we present the message of Islam, we must gauge spiritual readiness rather than worldly prominence. A poor, disabled, uneducated person whose heart trembles with awe of Allah is infinitely more receptive than a wealthy, influential person whose heart is encased in self-sufficiency. This divine calibration of priorities teaches that the words of the Qur’ān are a living reminder that yields its fruits only in the soil of a humble, mindful heart. The Prophet later embodied this lesson so completely that he would honour Ibn Umm Maktūm and appoint him as his deputy in Madinah, proving that the one who benefits from the reminder becomes a leader of the mindful.",
  "5": "After establishing the high rank of the eager seeker, the sūrah now turns to the other side of the incident, beginning with verse five: “As for the one who was indifferent.” This describes the attitude of the wealthy pagan leaders whom the Prophet (ﷺ) had been addressing.\n\nThe Arabic term used is istaghnā, which literally means to consider oneself self-sufficient or beyond need. It captures the spiritual disease of arrogance and pride, where a person feels no compelling need for guidance or purification. Such an individual relies on his wealth, status, or intellect and therefore becomes indifferent to the call of truth. The verse is carefully crafted; it does not mention the man’s name or tribe because his identity is irrelevant in the divine scale. What matters is his condition of heart. The Quraysh chiefs to whom the Prophet was speaking were steeped in their lineage and commercial success, and their indifference was a wall that prevented the reminder from penetrating. By drawing this contrast, the Qur’ān teaches that the call to Islam is not about chasing the powerful in the hope that they will bring others by their influence. Rather, the call is for those whose hearts are not yet encased in the delusion of self-sufficiency. The historical context shows the Prophet’s deep, merciful desire that these leaders embrace faith, a desire that was entirely in line with his compassionate nature.\n\nYet the Lord wanted to refine this prophetic zeal, ensuring that the message is never withheld from the humble while being excessively offered to the indifferent. This is a timeless principle: when someone exudes an attitude of “I have no need of your reminder,” the teacher must not invest all attention there to the neglect of the one who says “I am in desperate need.” The indifference of the elite is portrayed here not as a reason to abandon them entirely but as a reason to not make them the priority over the eager.",
  "6": "Verse six continues the description of the Prophet’s interaction with the indifferent pagan leader: “you gave him your undivided attention.” The phrase indicates a deliberate and complete turning toward the chief, a form of active engagement and hopeful persuasion. The Prophet was, after all, the most merciful and dedicated of teachers, and his eagerness to guide this man was driven by a profound concern for his eternal wellbeing.\n\nThe Arabic phrase taṣaddā conveys the sense of going out of one’s way, leaning in with focus. It shows the intensity of the prophetic effort in da‘wah. The verse is not a condemnation of engaging with disbelievers or people of influence; rather, it is a gentle correction of the allocation of that attention. The Prophet’s action was rooted in his perfect character, and the Qur’ān acknowledges this reality before subtly realigning it. There is a deep psychological and spiritual wisdom here. Often, well-meaning callers to truth invest tremendous energy trying to convince those who are hardened and indifferent, while neglecting sincere souls who are thirsting for even a drop of guidance. The divine instruction is not to ignore the indifferent completely but to never let their worldly stature cause the sincere to be pushed aside. The verb “gave you your undivided attention” also highlights the human limitation of the Prophet: he could not give equal simultaneous attention to everyone. Since he had to choose where to focus in that moment, the revelation indicates that his choice, though understandable, was not the most spiritually optimal one. It is an eternal reminder to weigh our priorities not by the noise or power of the audience but by the purity of their seeking. The one who trembles and asks should hear our voice before the one who turns away in scorn.",
  "7": "Verse seven brings a beautiful note of relief and clarity: “even though you are not to blame if he would not be purified.” This statement is inserted to protect the Prophet (ﷺ) from any misunderstanding of the previous correction. It makes clear that the Messenger’s duty is only to convey the message with clarity; he is not accountable for the ultimate purification of the listener. The indifferent chief, stuck in his self-sufficiency, may never be purified, and that outcome is not a failure on the part of the Prophet. The verse liberates the teacher from the crushing weight of results.\n\nIn the context of the incident, it is as if Allah is saying, “You turned your full attention to him, but you are under no obligation to ensure his purification.” The implication is profound: since his purification is not your responsibility, why then give him priority over the one whose heart is already inclined and whose purification is likely? The logic is gentle yet piercing. It reframes the entire purpose of da‘wah. The call to truth is an offering, not a negotiation. We are not salespeople trying to close a deal with the most prestigious client; we are gardeners watering the seeds that are already showing life.\n\nThe phrase also acknowledges a deeper theological reality: guidance is entirely in Allah’s hands. The Prophet (ﷺ) could not purify anyone; he could only provide the reminder that serves as a means. This verse thus reinforces the concept of tawakkul, reliance on Allah. The teacher does his duty, offers the reminder with wisdom, but leaves the outcome with the Almighty. This prevents burnout, despair, and misdirected efforts. It also corrects a common misconception that the Prophets had the ability to compel belief. They were human messengers, and this verse defends the Prophet’s honour by stating that even his intense attention would not be blamed had the chief remained impure, so his attention was better directed elsewhere.",
  "8": "In verse eight, the focus returns to ‘Abdullāh ibn Umm Maktūm with the words: “But as for the one who came to you, eager to learn.” The Arabic verb yas‘ā means he came striving, walking quickly, making an effort.\n\nThis is not a casual visit; it is an intentional journey of the seeker. The blind man’s physical movement toward the Prophet, despite his disability, mirrors the movement of his heart toward guidance.\n\nThe word “eager” captures his inner state: he did not come reluctantly or half-heartedly but with a palpable yearning to receive knowledge and purification. This description stands in sharp contrast to the “indifferent” one who was self-sufficient and felt no urgency. The Qur’ān is painting a picture of two archetypes: the arrogant one who stays away or listens with an air of superiority, and the humble one who rushes forward despite obstacles. This eager coming is, in itself, a sign of faith, because no one strives to reach a teacher of truth unless Allah has already placed light in their heart. The verse therefore exalts the very act of seeking sacred knowledge. It teaches that the student’s striving is honoured in the sight of Allah and should be honoured by the teacher. The Prophet, before this revelation, might have subconsciously valued the potential of the powerful man over the obvious need of the blind man, but the divine words recalibrate this completely. Now the standard is revealed: the one who comes to you, striving, regardless of his social station, is the one deserving of your welcoming attention. This verse is a charter of Islamic education, insisting that access to knowledge is a right for every eager heart, not a privilege for the elite. It also highlights the beauty of the Prophet’s later conduct, when he would spread his cloak for this very man, showing that the lesson had been absorbed fully into his noble character.",
  "9": "The ninth verse adds a critical inner dimension to the blind man’s eagerness: “being in awe of Allah.” The Arabic term is khashyah, a profound form of fear mixed with reverence, love, and deep knowledge of Allah’s majesty.\n\nThis is not the terror of a tyrant but the trembling of a heart that recognises the greatness of its Creator. ‘Abdullāh ibn Umm Maktūm did not come merely to satisfy curiosity or gain social standing; he came because his heart was saturated with awe of the Almighty. This quality is the very essence of beneficial knowledge. The verse indicates that the reason he was so eager to learn was precisely this awe; he sensed his own ignorance and the magnitude of what he did not know, and he feared standing before Allah without the guidance he desperately sought. This inner state makes a person profoundly receptive to the reminder. The Prophet’s frown, however slight, was therefore not a response to an ordinary interruption but to the approach of a heart filled with khashyah. The divine correction is all the more poignant because the Qur’ān is teaching that a person with khashyah is never an inconvenience; he is a blessing.\n\nThe verse also subtly defines the most important characteristic of a true student of the Qur’ān. Before the ears can truly benefit, the heart must be in a state of humble awe. This connects beautifully with other Qur’ānic verses that say only those who fear Allah will receive the reminder. By describing the blind man in this way, Allah is validating the man’s sincerity and showing that his interruption was spiritually motivated.\n\nFor every believer today, this verse serves as a mirror: when we approach the Qur’ān or sacred knowledge, are we coming with the casual attitude of the indifferent one or with the urgent awe of this noble blind companion, whose heart could see what many with eyes could not?",
  "10": "Verse ten delivers the direct point of correction with the words: “you were inattentive to him.” The Arabic talahhā means to be distracted or to turn away from one thing while occupied with another. The verse acknowledges that the Prophet’s inattention was not out of malice but out of his absorption with the chieftain.\n\nYet, in the economy of divine justice, even a moment’s neglect of a person with such awe and eagerness required a gentle but clear recalibration. The tone is not one of harsh punishment but of loving education. The Prophet (ﷺ) was being trained to become the most perfect guide for all humanity, and this training involved reorienting his natural human reflexes to align seamlessly with divine priorities.\n\nThe verse reveals that the one who possesses khashyah should never experience inattention from the carriers of the reminder. It underscores that the value of a human being is entirely tied to their piety and yearning for truth, not their sight, wealth, or influence. After this revelation, the Prophet completely internalised the lesson. When he later met ‘Abdullāh ibn Umm Maktūm, he would greet him with extraordinary warmth, saying, “Welcome to the one for whom my Lord rebuked me.” He would spread his own cloak for him to sit upon. This transformation shows that the Prophet’s character was a living embodiment of the Qur’ān; he did not resist the correction but embraced it as a gift from his Lord. For all Muslims in positions of teaching and leadership, this verse stands as an eternal reminder to examine who we might be unintentionally neglecting. Is there a sincere soul whom we have turned away from because we were busy with those who seem more important? The verse calls us to actively seek out and attend to the God-conscious, the humble, and the sincere, even if they come at an inconvenient moment. Inattention to them is a loss not to them alone but to the teacher who misses the blessing of their company.",
  "11": "With verse eleven, the sūrah transitions from the specific historical incident to a broader declaration about the nature of the revelation. It begins with the strong interjection “But no!\n\n” The Arabic kallā is a forceful negation, rejecting any idea that the preceding values might be neglected. It serves as a pivot, as if to say that the truth is far greater than the momentary human interactions just described.\n\nThe verse then states: “This revelation is truly a reminder.” Here, the specific message of the sūrah and the entire Qur’ān are identified as a tadhkirah, a powerful reminder that awakens the primordial covenant written in every soul.\n\nThe use of “truly” emphasises certainty and importance. The reminder is not a new, alien teaching but a reawakening of what human nature already recognises. By calling it a reminder, the verse connects the etiquette of teaching just outlined to the very essence of the revelation. The Qur’ān is not a book of mere information to be distributed to the highest bidder; it is a soul-stirring reminder that should be offered first to those whose hearts are trembling with awe. The incident with the blind man was a practical demonstration of how the reminder should be delivered, and now the sūrah elevates the reminder itself. It declares that this message is sufficient for all who wish to be mindful. The rebuke to the Prophet (ﷺ) is now placed in the context of the grandeur of the revelation. The Messenger is being taught that the weight and honour of the message he carries demand that he give it to those who reach for it, not chase those who run away.\n\nThis verse also acts as a bridge, reminding the reader that the ultimate purpose of these verses is not just to narrate a story but to serve as a living reminder for all generations, calling them to the same mindfulness and fear of Allah that the blind man possessed.",
  "12": "The logical consequence of the reminder’s nature is given in verse twelve: “So let whoever wills be mindful of it.” The word “so” links the availability of the reminder with the human response. The Qur’ān has been described as an honoured, clear reminder, and now the door is thrown open.\n\nThe phrase “whoever wills” (man shā’a) affirms human free will within the framework of divine decree. Allah has provided the reminder in its most perfect and accessible form, and He has endowed human beings with the ability to choose to be mindful. This verse balances the previous emphasis on divine guidance; while guidance ultimately comes from Allah, the individual must turn their will towards the reminder. The expression “be mindful of it” translates yadhdhakkarahu, meaning to actively remember, to take it to heart, to make it a continuous part of one’s consciousness. It is an ongoing, deliberate act, not a one-time acknowledgement. The verse thus places a profound responsibility on each person. No one can claim that guidance was kept from them; the reminder is clear and available. The blind man, despite his physical blindness, willed to be mindful, and he came striving. The pagan leaders, with all their faculties, willed to remain indifferent. The verse dismantles any excuse based on circumstance.\n\nIn the context of the sūrah, it is also a direct comfort to the Prophet (ﷺ). He does not need to exhaust himself chasing those who refuse to will; his task is to present the reminder with excellence and then allow each soul to make its choice. This verse liberates the caller from the burden of coercion. The invitation is made, the message is clear, and whoever truly wills to remember can do so. It is an empowering statement that places the accountability for disbelief squarely on the disbeliever’s own rejection of the reminder, while honouring the seeker who, like ‘Abdullāh, made the conscious choice to come forward.",
  "13": "The sūrah now draws our attention to the sublime nature of the reminder itself, beginning with verse thirteen: “It is written on pages held in honour.” This verse describes the physical or celestial record of the Qur’ān. The pages, or suhuf, refer to the scrolls upon which the revelation was recorded, and by extension, the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ) from which it was sent down.\n\nThe phrase “held in honour” translates mukarramah, meaning honoured, exalted, and dignified. These are not ordinary sheets of parchment; they are pages elevated in status because of the majestic words they bear. The honour of the pages stems entirely from the honour of the One who revealed them and the truth they contain.\n\nThis description serves multiple purposes. First, it emphasises the integrity and sanctity of the Qur’ānic text, countering any Meccan accusations that it was poetry or magic. Second, it reminds the Prophet (ﷺ) and the believers that the message they carry is so noble that it deserves to be treated with the utmost respect.\n\nThe earlier incident, where the Prophet was corrected for turning away from the blind seeker, is now placed in the shadow of these honoured pages. The teacher of this book must embody its honour by honouring every sincere student, regardless of worldly status. The imagery also inspires awe in the listeners: the book they hear recited is a transcript of heavenly pages.\n\nThus, the recipient should approach it with the same eagerness and awe that Ibn Umm Maktūm showed. The verse also connects to the Islamic tradition of showing deep respect for the muṣḥaf, the written copy of the Qur’ān, by handling it in a state of purity and with reverence. It is not a mundane object but a physical manifestation of honoured pages that angels themselves attend to.",
  "14": "Continuing the description of the sacred pages, verse fourteen says they are “highly esteemed, purified.” The word “highly esteemed” (marfū‘ah) conveys the idea of being raised high, both literally in the heavens and in status. This exaltation indicates that the Qur’ān is far above any falsehood or distortion; it occupies a rank that no human speech can attain.\n\nThe word “purified” (muṭahharah) adds a further dimension of absolute cleanliness and sanctity. No impurity, whether physical or spiritual, can touch its core essence. The pages are free from any satanic influence, error, or deviation. This purification also points to the fact that the true meanings of the Qur’ān are accessible only to those who are purified, as alluded to in Sūrah al-Wāqi‘ah. Together, these two qualities reinforce the transcendence of the revelation. They assure the believers that what they are receiving is of the highest heavenly standard, untainted by the disagreements and impurities of the earthly realm. For the Prophet (ﷺ), who had just been gently corrected, this description serves as an uplifting reminder that the message he is delivering is far above the petty dynamics of the Meccan chiefs. The status of the book he carries is so lofty that his own honour is inextricably linked to it, and his actions, in delivering it, must reflect its lofty purity.\n\nThis verse also carries a profound lesson for every reciter and student: the Qur’ān must be approached with a sense of its exaltation and with a heart purified from arrogance and distraction. The blind man came with a heart full of awe and a desire for purification, which made him the perfect recipient of a message described as purified and esteemed.\n\nThus, the qualities of the book mirror the required qualities of its true seeker.",
  "15": "Verse fifteen reveals the celestial agents through whom these honoured pages were transmitted: “by the hands of angel-scribes.” The term safarah, translated as “angel-scribes,” can also mean ambassadors or messengers. These are the angels who serve as intermediaries between Allah and His prophets, carrying the revelation from the Preserved Tablet to the earthly realm. The chief among them is Jibrīl (peace be upon him), who is described elsewhere as trustworthy and mighty.\n\nThe phrase “by the hands” personalises the transmission, indicating direct contact and meticulous care. The angels write and convey the message with absolute fidelity. This depiction counters the false claims of the Meccans that the Prophet (ﷺ) was taught by a mere human or that he received the revelation from devils. The true scribes are noble heavenly beings, not fallen spirits or poets. Their involvement elevates the status of the Qur’ān even further, connecting it to the realm of pure obedience and devotion.\n\nThe verse also indirectly teaches the virtue of the act of writing sacred knowledge. The angels themselves are scribes, recording the divine decree and the revelation.\n\nFor believers, this is an encouragement to write down, preserve, and honour the Qur’ān through the written word. The reference to hands does not imply human-like limbs for the angels; rather, it uses language understood by people to convey the care, skill, and direct involvement of these beings in the process of revelation. This entire beautiful imagery of honoured pages, high and pure, held by the hands of angelic ambassadors, instils a profound reverence for the book. It also serves as a contrast to the human interactions described earlier in the sūrah. The Prophet’s momentary inattention to the blind man is set against this cosmic backdrop of angelic precision and honour, highlighting that the message the Prophet delivers is not his own but a trust of unimaginable grandeur, and thus its distribution must be handled with the highest ethical standards.",
  "16": "The description of the angel-scribes culminates in verse sixteen with the words “honourable and virtuous.” The Arabic terms are kirāmin bararah. Kirām means noble, generous, and inherently good in character. Bararah refers to those who are devoutly obedient, righteous, and pure in their worship of Allah.\n\nThus, the angels who carried the revelation are not only of high rank but also of impeccable moral and spiritual integrity. There is no chance of betrayal, forgetfulness, or alteration. Their nature perfectly mirrors the qualities of the message they carry.\n\nThis verse also provides a profound lesson about the relationship between honour and virtue. In the Islamic worldview, true honour cannot be separated from righteousness and obedience. The angelic scribes are not just powerful; they are also deeply righteous. This serves as a model for anyone who handles the Qur’ān, whether through memorisation, teaching, or scholarship. One must strive to embody nobility of character and devotion to Allah, becoming a vessel worthy of the message.\n\nThe mention of these angelic qualities immediately after the incident with the blind man subtly draws a parallel: the Prophet (ﷺ), the human carrier of the Qur’ān, is being trained to exemplify the same virtues in his conduct. To be honourable is to welcome the sincere seeker; to be virtuous is to prioritise the fearful heart over the indifferent elite. By describing the angel-scribes in this way, the sūrah reassures the Prophet that he is in the company of the most noble beings in creation, even as he is lovingly guided. It also demolishes the pagan notion that the revelation could be the work of mischievous spirits. The bringers of the Qur’ān are models of piety, so the message can only lead to the highest human excellence. This verse etches into the believer’s heart the dignity and moral demand of interacting with the Word of God.",
  "17": "With verse seventeen, the sūrah shifts powerfully from the description of the glorious revelation to a sharp rebuke of the disbelieving human being. It declares: “Condemned are disbelieving humans! How ungrateful they are to Allah!\n\n” The Arabic opening “qutila al-insān” is a severe expression literally meaning “may he be killed” or “he is destroyed,” used here as a curse or a declaration of utter loss. It targets al-insān, the generic human being who persists in disbelief and ingratitude despite the overwhelming evidence of Allah’s mercy and power. The rhetorical question “How ungrateful they are!” (mā akfarah) expresses astonishment at the intensity of their rejection. What makes a human being, who has been granted life, intellect, and the honoured reminder carried by noble angels, respond with such profound ingratitude? The context of the sūrah provides a stark contrast: on one side stands the blind but grateful believer, ‘Abdullāh ibn Umm Maktūm, and on the other side stands the disbelieving human, steeped in denial. The ingratitude here is kufr, which originally means to cover up or to bury the truth. The human being covers the blessings of Allah with arrogance and refusal. This verse echoes the earlier description of the “indifferent” person but now universalises it to all who share that attitude. It is a powerful warning that the blessings of creation, sustenance, and revelation are so immense that rejecting them is an unimaginable betrayal.\n\nThe use of such strong language is meant to shake the listener awake. It challenges every person to examine their own response to the countless favours of their Lord, reminding them that ingratitude is not a small failing but the very root of spiritual destruction.",
  "18": "Following the condemnation of human ingratitude, the sūrah immediately begins to dismantle the arrogance that causes it by asking in verse eighteen: “From what substance did He create them?” This rhetorical question is the first step in a powerful argument that draws the human being’s attention to his utterly humble origin.\n\nThe question forces the ungrateful person to reflect on the very beginning of their existence. It is not asked because Allah does not know, but to compel the human being to acknowledge a truth that he often forgets. The substance referred to is not mentioned explicitly in this verse, leaving the listener in suspense and preparing the mind for the answer in the next verse. The purpose of the question is to induce humility. The one who struts around in arrogance and turns away from the reminder must pause and consider his own material beginnings. The creation of a human being from an insignificant, lowly substance stands as an undeniable proof of the Creator’s power and the creature’s inherent dependence. If the human being reflects honestly, he must admit that he did not create himself, nor did he come from something grand and self-sufficient. The verse thus connects back to the theme of the sūrah: those who are indifferent and feel self-sufficient (istaghnā) need to be reminded of their origin in a drop of sperm, something that would normally cause a person to feel humble and shy.\n\nThe question also serves as a transition from the high heavenly realm of the honoured pages and angel-scribes to the earthly realm of biological creation, showing that the same Lord who sends down revelation is the One who originates human life from the most humble beginnings. The contrast deflates human arrogance.",
  "19": "Verse nineteen answers the previous question with devastating precision: “He created them from a sperm-drop, and ordained their development.” The “sperm-drop” translates nuṭfah, which refers to a tiny, insignificant fluid that is the origin of human life.\n\nThis is a recurring Qur’ānic theme meant to humble human beings. The lowly nature of the starting material exposes the absurdity of human arrogance; the same person who now feels self-sufficient and turns away from truth was once a mere drop that could have been washed away and forgotten.\n\nThe second part of the verse, “and ordained their development,” translates faqaddarah, meaning He then proportioned, measured, and destined the stages of growth. From that single drop, Allah created a complex human being with limbs, senses, intellect, and a spiritual capacity. This ordination includes the genetic blueprint, the precise developmental stages in the womb, and the unique characteristics of each individual. The wording implies an intricate, purposeful design, not a random, chaotic emergence.\n\nThe phrase also carries the meaning of decreeing one’s life span, provisions, and deeds. So, not only does Allah create the physical form from a drop, but He also decrees the course of the person’s life. This double action—creation from a lowly origin and meticulous ordination—proves the power and wisdom of the Creator. For the ungrateful human, this verse is a mirror. If Allah brought you from a drop to a full human being, how can you be indifferent to His reminder? The contrast with the earlier verses about the honoured revelation is striking: the same Lord who sends down the most exalted pages from the heavens also carefully fashions a human being from the humblest of substances. Both actions testify to His mercy and deserve profound gratitude, not the arrogance of self-sufficiency and the rejection of His signs.",
  "20": "Verse twenty continues the description of Allah’s abundant favours upon the human being, stating: “Then He makes the way easy for them.” After creation and the ordination of development, the human being is not left to struggle alone. The “way” refers to the entire course of life that follows birth. The verb yassara means to make smooth, to facilitate, to ease. It applies to the physiological process of birth itself, which, despite its pains, is a facilitated passage out of the womb. It also extends to the journey of life: Allah has made the paths of sustenance, growth, and understanding easy. The infant finds the mother’s milk ready; the child’s intellect develops in stages that allow for learning; the natural world is subjugated for human benefit. Even the path to guidance is made easy for the one who sincerely seeks it, as demonstrated by the blind man ‘Abdullāh who was guided to the Prophet and received the reminder. The verse thus completes a triad of divine actions: creation from a humble drop, precise proportioning and decree, and the facilitation of life’s path.\n\nThis is the ultimate argument against ingratitude. The human being who is condemned in verse seventeen is the one who receives all these generosities and yet denies the Giver. The way being made easy also carries a moral and spiritual dimension. Allah has sent messengers, revealed books, and granted intellect so that the human being can easily recognise truth from falsehood. The indifference described earlier in the sūrah is therefore entirely unjustifiable. There is no insurmountable obstacle blocking the path to faith; the obstacles are created by the heart’s own arrogance. By mentioning this ease, the verse subtly invites the disbeliever to reconsider. If every step of your existence was made easy, will you not make easy your own heart’s turning toward the One who facilitated it all? It is a tender and logical call that completes the foundation for the next verses, which speak of death and resurrection.",
  "21": "After describing the ease with which Allah brings the human being into life and facilitates his path, verse twenty-one brings the first journey to a quiet close: “then causes them to die and be buried.” The transition marked by “then” moves from the active, facilitated life to its inevitable end.\n\nThe word “causes them to die” translates amātah, reminding us that death is not an accident or a mere biological failure; it is a deliberate act of the Creator who first gave life. Every soul experiences death by His decree, at a time and in a manner that no one can escape.\n\nThe second part, “and be buried,” refers to the honour Allah has bestowed upon human beings even after their souls depart. Unlike animals whose carcasses remain exposed, the human body is typically interred into the earth, covered and protected.\n\nThe Arabic fa-aqbarah implies not just the act of burial but the provision of a grave, a hidden resting place. This is a sign of divine regard, a final act of dignity for the human body that was once a humble sperm-drop.\n\nThe verse continues to dismantle arrogance; the same self-sufficient person who turns away from the reminder must now reckon with the reality that all his power and status will end in a silent grave. The mention of burial also evokes the physical return to the earth from which humanity was originally created, bringing the life cycle full circle.\n\nThis is not a pessimistic statement but a reminder of the transition to the next phase. It connects seamlessly to the earlier mention of creation from a drop and ordination, showing that the One who proportioned life’s beginning has also proportioned its end.\n\nFor the believer, it cultivates detachment from worldly vanity; for the disbeliever, it is a warning that the life he clings to will inevitably be cut short. The verse sets the stage for what follows: resurrection. It also indirectly refutes the pagan Arab notion that death is final annihilation; by saying “then causes them to die and be buried,” the Qur’ān implies a careful process that will later lead to being brought forth again.",
  "22": "Verse twenty-two provides the glorious culmination of the human journey: “Then when He wills, He will resurrect them.” The phrase “when He wills” places the timing entirely within Allah’s knowledge and decree.\n\nThis is not a continuous process but a single, decisive event that will occur at the exact moment appointed in the divine plan. The human being has no control over when this resurrection will happen; it is concealed wisdom.\n\nThe word “resurrect” translates anshara, which literally means to raise up, to bring out from the grave, to spread forth after being compacted. This is the ultimate undoing of burial. The One who caused death and the hidden grave will reverse the process and call every soul back to life. The verse serves as a direct answer to the Meccan disbelievers who mockingly asked, “Who will bring us back after we have become dust and bones? ” It asserts that just as the origin was a simple, forgotten drop, and just as the path of life was made easy, the return to life for judgment is equally simple for the Almighty.\n\nThe phrase “when He wills” also indicates that this resurrection is not a natural emergence but a deliberate act of sovereign will. It does not depend on natural laws but on the command “Be!” and it is. The connection to the preceding verses is profound: the One who created from a drop, ordained development, made the way easy, then caused death and burial, will certainly resurrect when He wills.\n\nThis is the logical completion of the argument against ingratitude. If the entire trajectory of human existence is in Allah’s hands, then denying the final resurrection is a selective blindness.\n\nMoreover, this verse brings hope and warning. For the believer who lived in awe, resurrection is the door to eternal joy; for the ungrateful one condemned earlier, it is the beginning of inevitable accountability. It anchors the moral weight of the surah, reminding all that the end of the story is not the grave.",
  "23": "Immediately after establishing the certainty of resurrection, verse twenty-three delivers a sharp verdict: “But no! They have failed to comply with what He ordered.” The strong opening “But no!” (kallā) is again a forceful negation, rejecting any assumption that the disbeliever has fulfilled his purpose.\n\nThe phrase “they have failed” translates lammā yaqḍi, which implies not yet having carried out, fulfilled, or completed a command. The command referred to here is the full range of divine obligations and the very purpose of creation: to recognise and worship Allah, to be grateful, to heed the reminder. Despite the clear signs of creation from a humble drop, the facilitation of life, the inevitability of death, and the promise of resurrection, the disbelieving human still neglects what he has been ordered. The verse does not specify a single command, making it inclusive of the entire religious and moral duty. Some scholars have also connected this to the specific obligation of reflecting on the signs leading to faith, while others mention the primordial covenant taken from the children of Adam. Regardless, the core meaning is that the human being has not risen to the trust he was given.\n\nThis statement echoes the earlier condemnation of human ingratitude and intensifies it. The one who was given so many means of ease has not even made the effort to comply with the most basic orders of his Creator.\n\nThe verse also carries a tone of regret and warning: despite all the stages of care—creation, guidance, death with dignity, and the promise of resurrection—people still fall short. It reminds the believer of the constant need for self-examination. Have we truly complied with what Allah has ordered, or are we among those who are described as not having fulfilled their duty? The verse thus bridges the argument from creation to the coming judgment, showing that the failure to comply now will have consequences when resurrection occurs. It reinforces the theme that knowledge and opportunity demand action.",
  "24": "In verse twenty-four, the surah introduces a new but deeply connected line of reflection: “Let people then consider their food.” The word “then” links the command to the preceding critique. Since the disbelieving human has failed to comply with what was ordered and remains ungrateful despite the signs in his own creation, he is now invited to reflect on a daily, tangible miracle: his food. The verb “consider” translates yanẓur, which means to look carefully, to examine, to contemplate with insight.\n\nThis is not a casual glance but a deliberate and thoughtful observation. Food is something every human being interacts with multiple times a day, yet few pause to trace its journey from the heavens to the plate. The verse universalises the invitation: “let people” includes every human being, believer and disbeliever alike. The food we eat is so familiar that its miraculous nature often escapes us. This verse employs a profound pedagogical technique, moving from the creation of the self to the provision of sustenance, leaving no excuse for heedlessness. The argument is that the same Lord who fashioned you from a drop and caused you to die is the One who provides the food by which you stay alive. If you cannot deny His hand in your own existence, how can you overlook His hand in the nourishment that reaches you every single day?\n\nThe verse also indirectly addresses the Meccan pagans who were largely an agricultural society surrounded by barren deserts and occasional fertile valleys. They knew the harsh reality of drought and the life-giving mercy of rain. By turning their attention to food, the Qur’ān is grounding their spiritual reflection in their immediate, practical experience. For the modern reader, the command remains equally urgent: to see in every meal a sign of the Creator’s meticulous care, and to respond with gratitude rather than the condemned ingratitude that marked the disbelieving human.",
  "25": "Verse twenty-five begins the detailed answer to the call to consider food, starting from the very first cause: “how We pour down rain in abundance.” The pronoun “We” is the majestic plural, drawing attention to the majesty and power of Allah. The act of pouring rain is described with the word ṣabb, which conveys gushing, copious, and forceful pouring. Rain is not a mere drizzle; it is sent in abundance to saturate the earth and create the conditions for life. This language emphasises divine generosity. Rain is also purely a mercy from Allah; no human can cause the skies to release water, and the ancient Arabs knew their utter dependence on rainfall for survival. The verse positions rain as the first link in the food chain, proving that human effort in agriculture is secondary and dependent. The farmer may plough and sow, but if the rain is withheld, all his labour is in vain. By highlighting rain, the Qur’ān directs the human gaze upward to the heavens, to the source of provision.\n\nThis is a powerful argument against polytheism; the pagans acknowledged that Allah was the ultimate provider of rain, yet they worshipped other deities. The verse implicitly asks: if Allah alone pours down the rain that grows your food, why do you turn away from Him?\n\nThe phrase “in abundance” also points to the delicate balance of the earth’s ecosystem. Too little rain causes famine; too much causes destruction. The abundance is measured perfectly to meet the needs of creatures. This care and precision are signs for those who reflect. The modern reader, with all the technology of irrigation and desalination, is still ultimately dependent on the water cycle set in motion by the Creator. The verse strips away human arrogance by reminding us that the very first step of our daily sustenance is entirely in divine hands, an act of pure mercy that should fill the heart with awe and gratitude.",
  "26": "Following the pouring of rain, verse twenty-six describes the next miracle: “and meticulously split the earth open for sprouts.” The Arabic verb shaqaqnā means We split, cleave, or crack open. It is paired with the word “meticulously” to reflect the precise, purposeful, and intentional nature of this action. After the rain saturates the soil, a profound transformation occurs. The seemingly solid, lifeless earth becomes soft and receptive, and then it literally splits apart as delicate green sprouts push their way through.\n\nThis is a powerful image of life emerging from death. The hard, dry ground that appeared barren and unyielding suddenly surrenders to the gentle force of a tiny seedling. It is one of the most common yet most easily overlooked miracles of daily existence. The verse uses this visual to prepare the mind for the concept of resurrection. The same Allah who splits the earth to bring out a living plant from a dead seed will split the graves to bring out human beings for judgment. The “sprouts” here refer to all forms of vegetation, the beginning of the plant life that will eventually provide grain, fruits, and fodder. By drawing attention to the splitting of the earth, the Qur’ān encourages human beings to observe the natural world not as a mechanical process but as a living sign of divine power. The meticulousness indicates that nothing is random; the type of plant that emerges is determined by the seed and the care of the One who created it. The crack in the soil is not a chaotic rupture but a perfectly designed emergence. The verse thus prepares the heart for the detailed listing that follows, creating a sense of wonder and anticipation. It also humbles those who see themselves as self-sufficient, reminding them that even the food they boast of comes from a process they did not initiate and cannot control.",
  "27": "The next verse narrows the focus from the general splitting of the earth to a specific, life-sustaining result: “causing grain to grow in it.” The grain mentioned here, ḥabb, includes all seed-bearing crops such as wheat, barley, rice, and other staples that form the foundation of human sustenance. The act of “causing to grow” translates anbatnā, from the root n-b-t, which indicates the sprouting and thriving of plants. It is attributed directly to Allah. The farmer casts seeds into the earth, but the actual growth—the swelling of the seed, the breaking of its shell, the upward reach of the stalk, the formation of the heavy grain head—is entirely beyond human power.\n\nThe verse reminds us that every grain is a sign of the Creator’s artistry. A single seed can produce a stalk bearing dozens of new grains, all containing the same latent potential for life. This multiplying miracle feeds civilisations.\n\nThe mention of grain also echoes the theme of resurrection, as the seed must “die” in its original form, buried in the soil, split open, before a new plant can emerge. This is precisely the process the disbelievers find so difficult to accept about their own resurrection, yet they witness it every harvest season.\n\nThe verse also connects to the practical reality of the Meccan audience who relied on grain caravans and local farming. By mentioning grain, Allah is pointing to something familiar and indispensable. The lesson is that the One who places this staple within the split earth is worthy of gratitude and worship. For the modern believer, this verse is an invitation to see every piece of bread as a verse of the Qur’ān manifest in nature. It should inspire not just a passing “Alhamdulillah” but a deep reflection on the chain of mercy that started with rain, moved through the splitting of the earth, and now results in the grain that sustains life.",
  "28": "Verse twenty-eight expands the list of provisions with a combination of delight and nourishment: “as well as grapes and greens.” The shift from grain to grapes introduces the element of pleasure and variety in Allah’s provision. Grain is the staple, the necessary sustenance, but grapes represent flavour, sweetness, and enjoyment.\n\nThe mention of grapes is particularly significant for the Arabs of the Hijaz, who valued vineyards and the fruit they produced, whether eaten fresh or dried as raisins. Grapes also symbolise a higher level of blessing, as they are not essential for bare survival but are gifts that beautify life.\n\nThe word “greens” translates wa-qadbā, which is commonly understood as fresh green fodder, alfalfa, or moist vegetables and herbs. This extends the provision to a wide range of fresh, green plant life that delights the senses and nourishes both humans and animals.\n\nThe pairing of grapes and greens highlights the diversity of the plant kingdom, a diversity designed to meet varied tastes and needs. The verse underscores that Allah did not merely provide monotonous subsistence but a colourful, flavourful, and textured provision that brings joy. This abundance is a clear proof against the ingratitude of the disbeliever. How can one enjoy the cool sweetness of a grape or the freshness of green herbs and not feel awe for the Creator who designed their seeds, their growth, and their taste?\n\nFurthermore, the mention of greens that are eaten directly without processing, alongside grapes that can be eaten fresh or transformed, shows the range of divine favour. For the contemporary reader, walking through a market and seeing the vivid colours of fruits and vegetables should trigger the same reflection: all these are grown from the same earth and watered by the same rain, yet each has a unique taste, shape, and nutritional profile, all by the meticulous design of the Almighty.",
  "29": "Verse twenty-nine continues to paint the landscape of divine provision: “and olives and palm trees.” These two trees hold a very special place in the Qur’ān and in the lives of the people of the Arabian Peninsula and the Levant. The olive tree is described elsewhere in the Qur’ān as blessed, and its oil is a source of light and nourishment. It is a tree of endurance, thriving in harsh, rocky environments and producing fruit that yields one of the purest oils. The olive’s mention here calls to mind both its immediate edible fruit and the extracted oil, which was a staple for cooking, lighting, and medicinal purposes. The date palm, nakhl, is the tree of life in the desert, offering not only its sweet fruit in various stages of ripeness but also trunks, leaves, and fibres for construction, shade, and weaving. By naming olive trees and palm trees together, the verse brings to attention two of the most generous and beneficial creations. Their produce is rich, sustaining, and storable, representing the long-term provision that secures communities against scarcity.\n\nThe pairing also connects the humid, fertile lands of the Mediterranean olive groves with the arid date-palm oases of Arabia, reminding the Meccans, who travelled north to Syria and south to Yemen for trade, of the varied landscapes Allah has created. The underlying argument is that if people reflect on these specific trees, they will see the careful handiwork of the Creator in their unique structures. A date seed, so hard and seemingly lifeless, splits open under the soil with the right conditions and pushes forth a shoot that eventually becomes a massive palm tree laden with clusters of sweet fruit.\n\nThis is a sign of resurrection as vivid as any. The verse thus urges the disbelieving human to look at the olive and the palm and see the trace of the One who fashioned him from a drop, then brought forth a complete being.",
  "30": "Continuing the rich visual catalogue, verse thirty adds “and dense orchards.” The Arabic word used is ḥadā’iq ghulban, a beautifully evocative phrase. Ḥadā’iq are walled gardens or lush orchards, with the root implying a rich, dark green colour, almost black in its intensity due to the density of the vegetation. Ghulban means thick, tangled, massive, and towering.\n\nThe image is of orchards so heavily planted with trees that their branches intertwine, creating a canopy of abundant shade and fruit. This description goes beyond single trees to an entire landscape of concentrated blessing. It conjures the oasis gardens that were the pride of Arabia, where multiple varieties of fruit trees grew together, providing not only food but also a cool, fragrant shelter from the harsh desert sun.\n\nThe term implies a deliberate planting and order, yet the word also acknowledges a certain majestic wildness and profusion. This verse shows that Allah’s provision is not merely functional but also breathtakingly beautiful. A dense orchard delights all the senses: the sight of layered green, the scent of fruit and earth, the sound of rustling leaves, the taste of fresh produce, and the touch of a cool breeze. All of this is presented as a direct result of the rain poured down and the earth split open, demonstrating that the same process that yields a single grain can also yield an entire orchard of interwoven abundance. For the ungrateful human, this lavish generosity is a shaming reminder. The Creator did not hold back His gifts but spread them in dense profusion.\n\nThe verse also encourages the believers to cultivate and appreciate the earth, seeing in a garden a reflection of the Paradise that awaits those who are grateful and mindful. The dense orchard is a foretaste of the gardens of eternity, a sign pointing from this world to the next.",
  "31": "The list reaches its comprehensive conclusion with verse thirty-one: “and fruit and fodder.” By mentioning “fruit” (fākihah) and “fodder” (abban), the verse covers the whole spectrum of plant-based provision intended for both humans and animals. Fruit is typically the sweet, enjoyable produce of trees and bushes, consumed directly for pleasure and nutrition. Fodder, here a broad term, refers to grasses, herbs, and pasture that serve as primary feed for livestock.\n\nThe pairing is highly instructive. Humans are not the only creatures in Allah’s design; animals are also under His care, and their sustenance is provided through the same process of rain and growth. This subtle inclusion reminds humanity of the interconnectedness of creation. The cattle, camels, sheep, and goats upon which the Arabs depended for milk, meat, wool, and transport are fed by the same mercy that feeds the human being.\n\nThus, the provision of fodder is indirectly a provision for human beings as well, since the well-fed animal becomes a source of immense good. The word “fodder” has also been interpreted by some scholars to include all kinds of herbage and vegetation not directly eaten by humans but essential to the ecosystem. This verse beautifully rounds off the reflection on food by showing that nothing is created in vain; even the most humble grass has a purpose in the divine economy. The sheer variety—grains, grapes, greens, olives, dates, orchards, fruits, fodder—demonstrates a generosity that defies the ingratitude condemned earlier. The human being is surrounded by a cascade of provisions, each a sign pointing back to the Giver.\n\nThe verse also subtly answers the potential arrogance of someone who might say, “I produced this food by my own labour.” The raw materials—rain, soil, seeds, growth—are all provided by Allah. The human role is merely that of a caretaker and harvester, not an originator.",
  "32": "The detailed catalogue of plant life concludes with verse thirty-two, which explicitly states the purpose: “all as a means of sustenance for you and your animals.” The word “sustenance” translates matā‘, which conveys the sense of provision, enjoyment, and benefit for a designated period. It emphasises that the bounties of the earth are a temporary gift, not an eternal possession. The explicit mention of “you and your animals” ties together the whole community of living beings under human care. The animals, referred to as an‘ām (livestock), are a blessing in themselves, and their sustenance is part of the blessing granted to human beings. This verse frames the entire discussion on food as a reminder that both human and animal life is upheld by a single, merciful Provider. It connects back to the condemnation of ingratitude: when Allah provides so comprehensively for every need, even for the beasts that serve humans, what excuse remains for arrogance and denial?\n\nThe verse also implicitly calls for responsible stewardship. If all this is a provision from the Creator, then it must be consumed with gratitude and without waste. The arrangement of the verses is deeply logical: from the fundamental act of rain, through the splitting of the earth, to the variety of crops, and finally to the declaration of their purpose. It is a complete journey from cause to final benefit.\n\nFor the disbeliever who questions the resurrection, the argument is clear: just as the dead earth is revived with vegetation to provide sustenance, the dead human bodies will be revived for judgment and eternal recompense. The sustenance of this world is a sign of the sustenance of the next. The verse leaves the human being standing amidst these blessings, called to respond not with the heedlessness of the indifferent one but with the eager mindfulness of the blind man who came seeking purity.",
  "33": "Having established the signs of creation, provision, and resurrection, the surah moves dramatically to the event of the Hour in verse thirty-three: “Then, when the Deafening Blast comes to pass.” The Arabic word for “Deafening Blast” is al-ṣākhkhah, one of the many names for the Day of Resurrection. Its root meaning conveys a violent, piercing noise that strikes the ears so intensely that it almost deafens. It is a single, unimaginable sound, the blowing of the Trumpet by the angel Isrāfīl, that will signal the end of the present order and the beginning of the resurrection.\n\nThe word “comes to pass” translates jā’at, indicating its absolute certainty and arrival at its decreed time. The use of such a terrifying auditory image is profoundly effective; no one can escape a sound that overwhelms the entire cosmos. It will silence every noise of this world, rendering all worldly concerns insignificant. This verse serves as a sharp turn in the surah. After the gentle, detailed description of the growth of food, the scene abruptly shifts to the apocalyptic event that renders all that provision irrelevant except as a source of accounting. The logical connection is clear: the One who demonstrated His power through the quiet miracle of a seed splitting the earth will also demonstrate His power through this overwhelming blast. The disbeliever who felt self-sufficient and indifferent is now being warned that a day will come when all his self-sufficiency will dissolve in an instant. The deafening nature of the blast symbolises the end of heedlessness; the arrogant will no longer be able to ignore the call of their Creator.\n\nFor the believer, this verse is a reminder to live with the awareness that the ṣākhkhah may arrive at any moment, turning the familiar world into a memory and ushering in the ultimate reality.",
  "34": "The surah now paints the human reaction to the Deafening Blast, beginning in verse thirty-four: “on that Day every person will flee from their own siblings.” The phrase “on that Day” anchors the following verses in the reality of the ṣākhkhah. The reaction is one of complete, terrified flight. The verb yafirru means to flee in panic, to run away with urgency and desperation. The astonishing detail is the object of flight: “their own siblings,” those brothers and sisters who were the closest companions in the worldly life. The bonds of siblinghood, forged through shared upbringing, blood, and affection, will be severed by the sheer terror of that moment. The verse indicates a complete breakdown of all social ties and natural affections. The person will not pause to inquire about their sibling’s safety; they will not seek to help or comfort them. Instead, they will run from them. This reversal of nature is a proof of the overwhelming horror of the Day. It is not a matter of hatred but of a terror so consuming that every soul will be entirely preoccupied with its own fate. The verse subtly answers the earlier arrogance of the disbelieving human who relied on his family, clan, and status. All these supports will vanish. Even the most intimate human relationship will be shattered by fear. The scene is a direct challenge to those who, in this world, prioritise family loyalties over their duty to Allah. The blind man who came seeking purification understood that his true need was not his tribe but his Creator. On that Day, the wisdom of his choice will become blindingly clear. This verse serves as an urgent wake-up call, reminding believers that the only bond that will avail is the bond of faith and righteous deeds.",
  "35": "The escalation continues in verse thirty-five: “and even their mother and father.” After siblings, the verse moves to the parents, who are the very source of one’s existence and typically the most beloved of all people.\n\nThe word “even” captures the shock of the statement. The mother’s womb that carried and nurtured, the father’s protection and provision—all these intimate ties are dissolved in the terror of the Day. In normal human life, a person would instinctively sacrifice everything for their parents, but on that Day, the natural instinct of filial devotion will be overtaken by a greater, more immediate survival instinct of the soul. This does not imply a loss of love but a complete paralysis of the usual caring response due to the magnitude of the event. Every person will be in a state of ultimate self-concern. The verse highlights the loneliness of the Day of Judgment; each soul stands alone before its Creator, stripped of all the relationships that provided comfort and identity in the world. The wisdom of mentioning the parents here is profound. In Meccan society, loyalty to ancestors and elders was a cornerstone of their social and religious life. The pagans often rejected the Prophet’s message by claiming they were merely following the way of their fathers. On the Day of the Deafening Blast, those same fathers will be fled from. All the arguments based on ancestral tradition will evaporate. The verse thus dismantles a core justification for disbelief and reinforces the absolute importance of individual accountability. The message for every believer is to love their parents with the love that draws them together toward Allah, not with a blind loyalty that competes with the truth. On that Day, only a heart that has lived in awe of its Lord will find peace.",
  "36": "The flight from human ties reaches its climax in verse thirty-six: “and even their spouse and children.” The spouse is the life partner, the source of intimacy, comfort, and shared secrets. The children are the delight of one’s eyes, the hoped-for continuation of one’s legacy, and the object of the strongest parental love.\n\nYet on that Day, a person will flee even from them. This is the final stage of the severance of worldly attachments. The Qur’ān is not saying that love will turn into hate, but that the overwhelming terror will make everyone intensely focused on their own salvation. The person will have no mental or emotional space to think of anyone else. This verse, along with the previous ones, forms a powerful parallel to Sūrah ‘Abasa’s earlier theme of priorities. The Prophet was gently corrected for prioritising the powerful pagan over the eager blind man. Here, the surah shows that on the Day of Judgment, all worldly priorities will be upended. The family ties that people often place above Allah will be of no benefit. The lesson is clear: while family bonds are sacred and must be maintained with kindness, they must never become a reason to turn away from the truth. The person who fled from the reminder in this world will, on that Day, flee from the very people he used as an excuse.\n\nFor the believer, the verse is a source of both solemn reflection and reassurance. It is a reminder to order one’s loves correctly, placing love of Allah and His Messenger at the pinnacle, and to raise one’s family in faith so that they are not fled from in terror but are reunited in the gardens of Paradise. The verse beautifully sets the stage for the next statement, which explains the reason for this universal flight.",
  "37": "After depicting the comprehensive flight from family, verse thirty-seven provides the rationale: “For then everyone will have enough concern of their own.” The Arabic phrase “sha’nun yughnīh” is deeply expressive. Sha’n means affair, condition, state, or concern. Yughnīh means it will occupy him fully, it will suffice him, leaving him in need of nothing else because he is entirely engrossed. The verse states that on that Day, every single person will be absorbed in their own personal state to such an extent that they will have no spare attention for anyone else, no matter how beloved. The cause of this total absorption is the overwhelming reality of standing before the Almighty for judgment. The self will be faced with its own record of deeds, its own fears, its own hopes. The normal human psychology of mutual concern will be suspended.\n\nThis is not a punishment but a necessary condition of that moment of truth. The individuality of the soul, which was so often buried under social relationships and tribal identities in this world, will stand forth naked and unassisted. The verse reinforces the Qur’ānic theme that no bearer of burdens will bear the burden of another. The believers are consoled by this because it means that the oppressors and the worldly powers cannot harm them on that Day; everyone is busy with their own account. Conversely, it warns those who assume that their family’s status or their ancestors’ piety will help them; all such interwoven chains of support are cut. This profound isolation is the ultimate manifestation of divine justice. The lesson for the living is to prioritise the state of one’s own soul before that day arrives. It calls for a healthy spiritual selfishness in seeking salvation, not at the expense of others but in recognising that one’s primary responsibility is one’s own standing before Allah. The blind man ‘Abdullāh understood this; his urgent concern was his own purification, and he sought it actively.",
  "38": "With the Day’s terror fully established, the surah now divides humanity into two categories based on their outcome. Verse thirty-eight describes the first group: “On that Day some faces will be bright.\n\n” The word “bright” translates musfirah, from the root safar, which means to shine, to become radiant, to be lit up with light. These faces are luminous with joy, relief, and the light of faith. The brightness is both a physical radiance, as mentioned in other verses where the believers’ light runs before them, and a visible expression of inner bliss. The faces of the saved will reflect the contentment of souls that have received their record in their right hands and know they are destined for eternal delight. The contrast with the previous flight from family is striking. While the disbelievers flee in panic, these bright faces are serene and hopeful. Their worldly relationships may also be a source of joy if those loved ones are also among the blessed, but the primary radiance comes from the individual’s own relationship with Allah. This verse rewards the mindfulness and awe that the blind man ‘Abdullāh carried in his heart. Those who were purified and benefitted from the reminder will have their inner purification manifested as outward radiance. The light on these faces is not a reflection of the sun but a gift from Allah, a direct result of their faith and righteous deeds. It is the visible sign of salvation. The verse provides immense hope to the believers who may suffer in this world, reminding them that a Day is coming when their faces will shine while the faces of their oppressors will be covered with dust. It also serves as an incentive to seek that brightness through following the teachings of the Qur’ān.",
  "39": "The state of the bright-faced ones is further elaborated in verse thirty-nine: “laughing and rejoicing.” The laughter mentioned here is not the mocking, arrogant laughter of the disbelievers in this world but the pure, joyful laughter of delight and relief.\n\nThe word ḍāḥikah conveys an open, genuine happiness. This is the spontaneous expression of a soul that has escaped the terror of Hell and has been granted the gardens of bliss.\n\nThe phrase “and rejoicing” translates mustabshirah, which implies a face aglow with the expectation of good news, beaming with pleasure. They are rejoicing at the immense reward, the mercy of their Lord, and the beautiful companionship of Paradise. This verse provides a dramatic emotional release after the intense description of the Deafening Blast and the flight from family. It tells us that the Day of Judgment, for all its terror, culminates in a joy that is beyond human imagination for the righteous. The laughter and rejoicing are not restrained; they are the full, overflowing happiness of a heart that has achieved its purpose.\n\nIn the context of the surah, this is the fruit of being eager to learn and having awe of Allah. The one who came to the Prophet with urgency and fear is, on that Day, the one laughing and rejoicing. The worldly roles are reversed. Those who were considered lowly or inconvenient in this world become the radiant, joyous ones. This verse should inspire every believer to seek that laughter, a laughter that is never again tainted by sorrow. It reminds us that the trials and the moments when faith required sacrifice were all worth it, because they lead to this face beaming with joy. The Prophet, who lovingly accepted the divine rebuke, will be the foremost of those laughing and rejoicing, along with the companions who followed his corrected example.",
  "40": "The stark contrast is presented in verse forty: “while other faces will be dusty.” The conjunction “while” sets up the division of humanity on that Day. The “other faces” belong to the disbelievers and the wicked.\n\nThe word “dusty” translates ‘alayhā ghabarah, meaning a coating of dust, like a layer of dirt that covers the skin. This dust is the physical manifestation of their humiliation, gloom, and exhaustion. In stark opposition to the bright, shining faces of the believers, these faces are darkened, soiled, and weary. The dust suggests both the aftermath of a catastrophic collapse—as if they have been crushed and coated with the debris of the world—and the internal darkness of their hearts made visible. The pristine honour they clung to in the world is gone. This verse directly connects back to the earlier condemnation of the ungrateful human being. The one who was indifferent and self-sufficient, who turned away from the reminder, will stand on that Day with a face covered in dust. The imagery is deeply humbling. Wealth, beauty, status, and tribal pride will be replaced by a coat of misery. The dust also symbolises the sterility of their deeds. Just as dust yields no fruit, their worldly actions, which were not done for Allah, are utterly worthless. The verse serves as a powerful warning. It encourages a person to look in the mirror of the soul and ask which face they are preparing for that Day. The surah that began with a gentle frown now concludes with this powerful visual separation of ultimate fates. The dust-covered face is the direct outcome of failing to comply with what Allah ordered, of refusing to consider one’s food and its Provider. It is the visible sign of a lost soul, a warning that should stir the heart to seek the purification and mindfulness that lead to brightness instead.",
  "41": "The description of the condemned faces reaches its final depth in verse forty-one: “cast in gloom.” This short but weighty phrase adds a new dimension to the dusty faces mentioned just before.\n\nThe word “gloom” translates qatarah or tarhaquhā qatarah, which conveys a covering of darkness, blackness, and abasement that overcomes the face. If the dust represented the exhaustion and worthlessness of their deeds, the gloom represents the inner despair, humiliation, and sorrow made visible. The face is not just soiled with external dirt; it is darkened by a deep, spiritual blackness. This darkness is the opposite of the radiant light on the faces of the believers. In many Qur’ānic descriptions of the Last Day, the believers’ light will shine before them and on their right, while the hypocrites and disbelievers will be plunged into darkness. Here, the gloom is directly upon their faces, as if they are already being enveloped by the shadows of Hell.\n\nThe word “cast” implies that this gloom is a covering thrown over them, not a natural complexion. It is a punishment, a visible mark of their disgrace. The verse forces the listener to visualise the stark contrast: one group is laughing and rejoicing with bright faces, while the other is covered in dust and darkness. This gloom is not merely physical; it is a reflection of the state of their hearts in the world. Their hearts were blind to the truth, and on that Day, the blindness manifests as a visible darkness on their faces. The surah thus completes the physical and emotional portrait of the losers. It is a terrifying warning that urges every soul to seek the purification and mindfulness mentioned in the earlier verses, so that they may not be among those whose faces are overtaken by this gloom. The brevity of the verse heightens its impact, leaving the mind to contemplate the horror of that eternal shadow.",
  "42": "The surah concludes by identifying the people of the dusty, gloomy faces with absolute clarity in verse forty-two: “those are the disbelievers, the wicked sinners.” After the vivid imagery of the Day of Judgment, the revelation now explicitly names the two defining characteristics that led to such a terrible end: disbelief (kufr) and wicked, sinful behaviour (fujūr).\n\nThe term “disbelievers” (al-kafarah) refers to those who actively covered up the truth, who rejected the reminder despite its clarity, and who persisted in the indifference and self-sufficiency condemned earlier in the surah. The second term, “the wicked sinners” (al-fajarah), comes from the root fajara, meaning to split open or to plunge into sin. It describes those whose moral restraints are shattered, who indulge in immorality, transgression, and defiance of Allah’s commands without remorse.\n\nThe pairing of these two descriptors is critical; it shows that disbelief and sinful conduct are inseparable. The one who rejects faith inevitably leads a life of wickedness, because without the light of revelation, there is no moral compass. Conversely, those who persist in wickedness often end up burying their innate sense of truth, becoming disbelievers. The surah thus ends with a clear division of humanity based on their response to the reminder. On one side are the believers, whose faces are bright and rejoicing; on the other are the disbelievers and wicked, whose faces are dusty and gloom-covered. The entire surah can be seen as a journey from the gentle divine correction of the Prophet regarding a sincere seeker, through the signs of creation and sustenance, to this final, stark outcome. It reminds us that the indifference of the elite and the arrogance of the self-sufficient will result in this ultimate disgrace, while the eagerness and awe of a blind believer will result in radiant joy. The surah’s closing verse leaves no ambiguity; there is no middle ground on that Day. It is a direct call to examine one’s own state: are we among those who honour the reminder, or among those who turn away in self-sufficiency?"
};
